@node EN_Intro "What it is"
@{b}What it is@{ub}

AmiHomeassist shows your smart home on the Amiga: lights, sockets,
blinds, thermostats, windows, sensors and energy meters from Home
Assistant, arranged on dashboards of your own.

On the left a sidebar with one page per room (or whatever you like), on
the right the contents of the chosen page in framed groups. One click on
a switch flips the device. States are polled, so changes made elsewhere
show up by themselves.

A large Home Assistant easily has 900 entities; you want a dozen or two
on the Amiga. You pick them, and an editor lets you arrange pages,
groups and devices freely - by mouse, with drag and drop.

@{" Picture: main window " link "Pics/main.ilbm/main"}

AmiHomeassist is careful with the wire: the full device catalogue is
fetched once, after that only the states of the devices on screen. In a
setup with 941 entities that is 1.4 KB per poll instead of 55 KB.
@endnode


@node EN_Need "What you need"
@{b}What you need@{ub}

 - AmigaOS 2.0 or newer, 68020 or better. No FPU needed.

 - MUI 3.8 or newer.

 - The NList classes NList.mcc and NListview.mcc. MUI does NOT ship
   them. Get them from Aminet (dev/mui/MCC_NList.lha) or from
   https://github.com/amiga-mui/nlist and put them in MUI:Libs/MUI.
   Check with:  Version MUI:Libs/MUI/NList.mcc

 - A TCP/IP stack with bsdsocket.library: Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami ...

 - Home Assistant reachable over plain http. https is not supported
   and is refused with a clear message.

 - A long-lived access token from Home Assistant: your profile, tab
   Security, at the bottom under "Long-lived access tokens".

Tested on an A500 with a Terrible Fire TF536 (68030, no FPU, plipbox)
and on an A500 with PiStorm, both with OS 3.2 and MUI 3.8.
@endnode


@node EN_Setup "Setting up"
@{b}Setting up@{ub}

 1. Copy the AmiHomeassist drawer wherever you like.

 2. Start AmiHomeassist. On the first run the @{"settings" link EN_Settings}
    window opens by itself. Enter address and token, then Save.

 3. The device picker opens with a suggestion. It is only a guess -
    look it over, adjust, then Apply. One page per room is created.
    See @{"Choosing devices" link EN_Select}.

 4. Want it different? Use "Edit ..." - see @{"The editor" link EN_Editor}.

The program sets up its own stack of 64 KB, so the stack entry in the
icon does not matter.
@endnode


@node EN_Window "The main window"
@{b}The main window@{ub}

@{" Picture: main window " link "Pics/main.ilbm/main"}

Left: the logo with the version, below it your pages. Click a page to
show it on the right.

Right: the page, in framed groups. Labels stand right-aligned with a
colon in front of their value, so everything lines up. Switches flip
on a click, blinds and thermostats have buttons.

At the bottom:

   Refresh      fetches everything anew - catalogue, pages, charts
   Select ...   which devices the program knows, see @{"Choosing devices" link EN_Select}
   Edit ...     arrange pages, see @{"The editor" link EN_Editor}
   Settings ... address, token, interval, see @{"Settings" link EN_Settings}

The status line shows how many devices sit on how many pages, or what
went wrong.

On start the window appears only when all pages are built - on a slow
Amiga that takes two or three seconds.
@endnode


@node EN_Kinds "Widget kinds"
@{b}Widget kinds@{ub}

   Switch        light and switch - the toggle you click
   Indicator     binary_sensor - window open or closed
   Number        sensor with its unit
   Bar           sensor with a fixed range, per cent or watts
   Blind         cover - up, stop, down, and the position
   Thermostat    climate - current and target temperature, colder,
                 warmer, operating mode
   Text          a caption
   History       bar chart of the last 30 days or 12 months, see
                 @{"History charts" link EN_History}

When you add a device the program suggests a kind from its domain and
device class. The editor lets you change it.
@endnode


@node EN_Select "Choosing devices"
@{b}Choosing devices@{ub}

"Select ..." in the main window lists everything Home Assistant knows.
A click on a row ticks it or unticks it.

@{" Picture: device selection " link "Pics/select.ilbm/main"}

 @{b}Search@{ub}   Type into the search field and the list shrinks to the
          matches - in room, name and entity ID, upper or lower case
          does not matter. "All", "None" and "Suggest" then act only on
          what the list shows: search "grid", press "All", and you get
          all grid sensors, not the whole house.

 @{b}Suggest@{ub}  Ticks the usual devices and leaves out the ballast: the
          per-device internet switches of a Fritzbox, the Dnd lamps of
          smart sockets, anything unavailable.

 @{b}Apply@{ub}    Takes the ticks over into your pages. Newly ticked devices go
          onto the page of their room, into the group of their kind -
          both are created if missing. Unticked devices disappear. Your
          own arrangement stays as it is. History charts are never
          removed here, only in the editor.

Closing the window without Apply discards the changes.

@{b}More than 100 devices@{ub} make every poll large, and an A500 cannot keep
up. Apply then asks first:

@{" Picture: the question " link "Pics/manydevices.ilbm/main"}
@endnode


@node EN_Editor "The editor"
@{b}The editor@{ub}

"Edit ..." opens the editor on the page you are looking at.

@{" Picture: editor " link "Pics/editor.ilbm/main"}

Left the pages, right the contents of the chosen page: group headings
in bold, below them the devices with their kind.

 @{b}Pages@{ub}      New, Remove, Up, Down.
 @{b}Contents@{ub}   Group adds a group, Device ... adds a device, Remove,
            Up, Down.
 @{b}Selection@{ub}  Name and Rename; the page icon (with preview) and the
            widget kind, each confirmed with "set".

@{b}Drag and drop:@{ub} drag a device to another place or into another group,
a whole group by its heading, a page within the page list. Up and Down
do the same from the keyboard.

@{b}Device ...@{ub} opens the device picker. Start typing right away - the
cursor sits in the search field. A double click adds the device.

@{" Picture: adding a device " link "Pics/adddevice.ilbm/main"}

The main window follows the editor: after closing it you are on the
page you last worked on.

Nothing is written to disk until you press @{b}Save@{ub}. Before saving, the
old file is kept as Dashboards.prefs.bak - see @{"Files and backups" link EN_Files}.
@endnode


@node EN_History "History charts"
@{b}History charts@{ub}

@{" Picture: energy page with charts " link "Pics/energy.ilbm/main"}

A bar chart of the last 30 days or 12 months, like the statistics cards
in Home Assistant - for your power consumption, for instance.

How to make one:

 1. Edit ...  -> choose the page
 2. Device ... -> search for the meter, e.g. "grid home" -> double click
 3. click the new row, set the kind to "History (months)" or
    "History (days)", press "set"
 4. Save

@{b}Which sensors?@{ub} Only those with long-term statistics in Home
Assistant, that is with a state_class - energy meters in kWh, gas,
water. Power sensors (W, kW) only keep averages; they show "No data" -
use Number or Bar for them instead.

The values come from Home Assistant's long-term statistics over its
WebSocket interface. One query is 1 to 3 KB and takes about half a
second on a 68030. Charts are fetched at start, on Refresh and every ten
minutes. The Amiga clock does not matter - the date comes from Home
Assistant.

The number of bars can be changed in Dashboards.prefs, see
@{"Files and backups" link EN_Files}.
@endnode


@node EN_Settings "Settings"
@{b}Settings@{ub}

@{" Picture: settings " link "Pics/settings.ilbm/main"}

   Address          http://homeassistant:8123 - a numeric address
                    saves the name lookup
   Token            the long-lived access token
   Interval (s)     how often states are fetched. Over a slow link
                    15 or 30 seconds.
   Unknown values   what to do with devices that report "unknown" or
                    "unavailable": show them, grey them out (default)
                    or hide them. A group left empty disappears too.

If Home Assistant does not answer, the program gives up after five
seconds and then only knocks every thirty seconds until it answers
again - it never freezes.
@endnode


@node EN_Languages "Languages"
@{b}Languages@{ub}

English, German, Italian and Spanish. English is built in, the others
are in the Catalogs drawer. The language follows Prefs/Locale. For this
program only:

   SetEnv SAVE AmiHomeassistLanguage deutsch

Back to the system language - both lines:

   UnSetEnv AmiHomeassistLanguage
   Delete ENVARC:AmiHomeassistLanguage

Names of devices, rooms and units come from Home Assistant and are
already in your language.
@endnode


@node EN_Shell "From the shell"
@{b}From the shell@{ub}

   AmiHomeassistCLI                    list by room
   AmiHomeassistCLI ALL                including hidden entries
   AmiHomeassistCLI DOMAIN=sensor      one domain only
   AmiHomeassistCLI STATES             only the adopted devices
   AmiHomeassistCLI ON=light.kitchen
   AmiHomeassistCLI OFF=switch.beamer
   AmiHomeassistCLI TOGGLE=light.table
   AmiHomeassistCLI HISTORY=sensor.energy          last 30 days
   AmiHomeassistCLI HISTORY=sensor.energy MONTH    last 12 months
   AmiHomeassistCLI HISTORY=sensor.energy COUNT=7  last 7 days

Setting up without the interface:

   AmiHomeassistCLI HOST=http://homeassistant:8123 TOKEN=<token> SAVE
@endnode


@node EN_Files "Files and backups"
@{b}Files and backups@{ub}

   ENVARC:AmiHomeassist/AmiHomeassist.prefs   address, token, interval
   ENVARC:AmiHomeassist/Import.prefs          the ticked devices
   ENVARC:AmiHomeassist/Dashboards.prefs      the pages

Before each save the previous file is kept as .bak - Dashboards.prefs.bak
and Import.prefs.bak. If something went wrong, copy it back:

   Copy ENVARC:AmiHomeassist/Dashboards.prefs.bak
        ENVARC:AmiHomeassist/Dashboards.prefs

All files are plain text. A line in Dashboards.prefs:

   chart sensor.energy "Energy per month" 1 12

1 means months (0 days), 12 is the number of bars.

The token is stored in the clear and grants full access to your Home
Assistant. On your own network that is acceptable, but you should know
it.
@endnode


@node EN_Tips "Tips and trouble"
@{b}Tips and trouble@{ub}

 @{b}Slow?@{ub}  Keep the number of devices small - a few dozen are fine on an
        A500, several hundred are not. Raise the interval to 15 or 30
        seconds.

 @{b}"Home Assistant does not answer"@{ub}  Check the address in Settings.
        https:// does not work, http:// does.

 @{b}"Home Assistant rejects the token (401)"@{ub}  Create a new long-lived token in Home
        Assistant and enter it again.

 @{b}A device shows "missing"@{ub}  It no longer exists in Home Assistant.
        Remove it in the editor.

 @{b}Empty chart, "No data"@{ub}  The sensor keeps no long-term statistics;
        see @{"History charts" link EN_History}.

 @{b}Pages gone after an accident@{ub}  Restore Dashboards.prefs.bak, see
        @{"Files and backups" link EN_Files}.
@endnode


@node EN_Credits "Credits and rights"
@{b}Credits and rights@{ub}

Author: radi777. Source: https://github.com/radi7777/AmiHomeassist

MIT licence, source included in the Source drawer. muistubs.c comes
from the amimcp project and is Apache 2.0.

Icons: Material Design Icons, Apache 2.0. The house logo belongs to the
Home Assistant project, which is not affiliated with this program.

This program is vibe coded. I described what it should do, an AI wrote
the code, and I read, tested and decided what stayed in. The full source
is included so you can judge for yourself.
@endnode


@node EN_Versions "Version history"
@{b}Version history@{ub}

 @{b}0.9.1@{ub}  Search in both device lists. Apply adds and removes instead
        of rebuilding all pages; a question before more than 100
        devices; automatic .bak files. Crash when removing a page
        fixed. The window no longer closes and reopens on start. This
        guide.
 @{b}0.9@{ub}    History charts. Freeze on quit after a Workbench start fixed.
 @{b}0.8@{ub}    Tidier pages, unknown values, drag and drop in the editor,
        stack fix for real 68030 machines.
 @{b}0.7@{ub}    Thermostats, timeouts on the wire.
 @{b}0.6@{ub}    Four languages, cross-compiled with gcc.
 @{b}0.5@{ub}    First Aminet release: sidebar and dashboards.

The full list is in Changelog.txt.
@endnode


